WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:01.740
Good morning.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.290
Mike, you set a new record, 380 days in space to beat you.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:46.360
Well, the Russians have had people in space for up to 800 days in their career,

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:52.400
so I've got a long way to go, but I'm sure there's other people who are going to fly in our office,

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.000
more than one long-duration space mission, and I hope my record is soon broken,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.980
and I hope it just becomes kind of a ripple in the...

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:06.110
whole scheme of history as humans go on, Americans go on to explore the rest of the solar system.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.930
But in the meantime, I'm glad to share the moments with these guys.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:16.960
All right, you astronauts, you got about to explain to me exactly what the alpha-magnetic

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.400
spectrometer trying to find the origins of the universe.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.540
Well, you know, there's some really amazing things that it's trying to figure out.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.980
You know, Hubble for us, one of the important things.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.580
Hubble figured out was that the universe is not only expanding, but at an accelerated

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:34.040
rate.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:39.180
And our understanding of how that could be with mass, you know, when mass tracks and

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.700
should be pulling things back together, we clearly don't understand the mass that's

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.080
out there.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.000
And about 4 percent of the mass is the matter that we understand, and then there's other

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.680
kinds of matter that we're trying to figure out, and one of those we call dark matter

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.700
because physicists don't really understand what it is.

00:01:59.370 --> 00:01:59.870
the universe is.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.540
the universe we live in, how it all got here, how it was formed, these are fundamental questions

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:08.140
and this is what AMS is going to be trying to understand for us.

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:14.910
My first of four space lights was as Endeavour's pilot on STS-108 in December 2001.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:21.550
Our mission is returning from the International Space Station after Endeavour's 12th visit to the orbiting laboratory.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:29.900
There, people from many nations have served continuously in successive crews since November of 2000, teaching.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.200
and learning from one another about interactions and about how best to do things in this harsh environment.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:38.020
If you ask me,

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:39.080
What I think.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:40.740
Endeavor's legacy would be.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.370
I'd have to say, well, first being a part.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.190
of the mainstay

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:47.830
the workhorse of the space program.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.450
the space shuttle for the last 30 years.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.420
Endeavor was a big part of that.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:54.010
but also Endeavor.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:57.430
had the unique distinction of bringing up the very first

00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.470
piece of the International Space Station.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:00.460
And then.

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:03.810
by installing the orbiter boom.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:05.810
system on top

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.880
for use for the station's arm to extend its robotic arm.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.840
The Endeavor brought up the last piece.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:16.680
of the space station as well. So I think Endeavor

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:21.100
legacy would be that she was a very important part of the

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.600
Assembly of the space station from beginning

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:25.060
to end.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:26.530
and also

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.960
to me personally because I had my first date on Endeavor.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.460
and I'm having my last date with Endeavor.
